 ## Overview
-Adaptive Refinement for Volume Rendering is an image-order technique that progressively improves visual fidelity by casting rays non-uniformly based on local image complexity, balancing rendering quality and performance in interactive settings [S3]. It sets itself apart from its siblings by either using a feedback loop to maintain a target frame rate while bounding error per texel [S1] or by employing a rigorous Finite Element Method (FEM)-based error estimator to guide h-adaptive subdivision of the image plane [S2, unofficial], whereas [Adaptive Screen Sampling](5838-adaptive-screen-sampling.md) typically relies on variance-based metrics and [Adaptive Ray Shooting & Quadtree Subdivision](8158-adaptive-ray-shooting-quadtree-subdivision.md) often focuses on object-space or hybrid approaches. Builds on [Adaptive Sampling and Resolution](1412-adaptive-sampling-and-resolution.md).
